{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed mechanism conflates 'compliance-tracking' with 'spurious-cue reliance,' yielding an invalid test. A checklist could be causally necessary and sufficient for the outcome, yet compliance-tracking is still 'mere' if the panel is merely executing steps it does not understand. Decorrelating checklist-compliance from the outcome is then impossible, so the target's binary is internally inconsistent: if the checklist items are genuine, decorrelation cannot be constructed; if they can be decorrelated, the checklist is spurious and survival must come from extra-checklist judgment, not a 'deeper mapping.' There is no third case in which decorrelation is feasible AND the checklist is non-spurious AND survival demonstrates genuine task-discrimination. The panel's only permitted behavior—following an explicit checklist—cannot produce a non-compliant accuracy path, so any survival under decorrelation necessarily implicates off-checklist judgment the target fails to distinguish from 'genuine' reliability.","target":"69539d4f8f882c1c8acebbb61099e450775cb8320901f1ef4830f77d048ff52c"}]}